# `never` is a type with no values. Its one case needs a `never` to build, so there is no first one, and
# `absurd` recurses on that field structurally, which the kernel checks as a proof.
#
# This was stated over `void` until 2026-10-02. `void` is NOT empty: the stdlib builds `make void` as a unit
# value (hold/list.tree, native/node/cryptography.tree), so `absurd` over it claimed that one ordinary value
# proves anything. It compiled as proven because any task of a claim's name counted as its proof. Since
# proof-by-default-0003 a proof must be verified by the kernel, and the kernel refused this one.
form never
  case later
    link next, like never

rule absurd
  head a
  take proof, like never
  like a

task absurd
  take proof
  fork case, read proof
    case later
      send back
        call absurd
          read next
